{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1368675-what-does-do-look-like","id":1368675,"title":"What does Do look like?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-17T21:01:47-08:00","id":1368675,"title":"What does Do look like?","content":"I've always loved the idea of the Akashic's kung fu wizards, but what does their kung fu actually look like? For those of you who have played around with Do, how do you visualize its practitioners fighting? At first I thought it was as simple as weaving different arts together, but that feels off the more I think a out it.<br /> <br /> What can you point to and say &quot;THAT'S what Do looks like&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/899-the-free-man","name":"The Free Man","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar899_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-17T22:57:33-08:00","id":1368688,"title":"","content":"Do is water, it follows the form of the vessel it's in. This isn't just a silly mocking of some of the more cringe-worthy pop culture kung fu bits, but actually something the books establish. Do is part of Akashic magical practice, and it's full expression is as individualistic as each mage. At low levels Do looks like a &quot;normal&quot; martial art likely reflecting the style(s) the mage already knows as they are still learning to expand their skills. Soon the influences of different schools start to manifest. <br /> <br /> The Kannagara's forms are more traditional and precise, as keepers of the ancient teachings of the Tradition, looking the most like the wuxia films. The Vajrapani are close to the Kannagara, but mix in &quot;new&quot; techniques from cultures other than those that the Akashayan consider direct cultural descendants of Meru; especially formal weapon based styles instead of purely bare-handed practice. The more Legalistic Shi-Ren are more pragmatic and brutal, swiftly crushing enemies to put things in their proper place (or putting parts of their foes in improper places) as they stamp out impropriety literally. The Jnani are the most esoteric in form, incorporating ritual practices of local shamans, traditional music and dance guiding the rhythms of their fighting. The Li-hai mix things up the most, finding Do in the least formal sources, mixing styles, using modern arts (both martial and other), but with a focus on non-lethal applications.<br /> <br /> Then they start to move beyond those stereotypes as they realize Do that resonates with their individual practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-18T15:17:10-08:00","id":1368807,"title":"","content":"It looks like this!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cnaeIAEp2pU\" data-code=\"cnaeIAEp2pU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/cnaeIAEp2pU/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-18T15:23:16-08:00","id":1368808,"title":"","content":"Do is really more of a way of life than a martial art. It literally means &quot;Way&quot; and for the Akashic is the Way toward enlightenment and Ascension. It encompasses everything a person does, how they act, how they think, how they live, and how they understand reality. Its eight areas of focus, called Limbs, are:<ul><li>Dhyana (meditation and awareness)</li> <li>Prajna (ethics and compassion)</li> <li>Karma (attentiveness and devotion)</li> <li>Sunyakaya (silence and listening)</li> <li>Dharmamukti (physical confrontation and evasion, including hand-to-hand combat)</li> <li>Shastamargat (precision and tools, including weapons)</li> <li>Tricanmarga (balance within the self and knowledge of one's place in the cosmos)</li> <li>Jivahasta (healing and medicine)</li> </ul>These apply to everything from being a master surgeon or brilliant manga artist to being a mountain dwelling shaman hermit or champion karate fighter. [Mechanically, each of these Limbs has a number of skills tied to it, and cultivating those skills is how a character cultivates that Limb.]<br /> <br /> As far as what we usually think of when hearing the term &quot;martial art&quot; goes, the five main factions of the Akashic Brotherhood each have their own &quot;style&quot; of exercise and disciplines built around their focus. <br /> <br /> <i>Lohan Chuan</i> (&quot;Monk Fist&quot;) is used by the Kannagara (monks, philosophers, and historians) to push the human body to its limits in order to show that those limits are just an illusion of Maya. It involves arduous physical exercise, acrobatics, incredibly complex Yoga postures, ritual chanting, and strict dietary regimes, with little in the way of actual combat. <br /> <br /> <i>Hsien Chuan</i> (&quot;Immortal Fist&quot;) is used by the Shi-Ren (benevolent aristocrats, legalists, and bureaucrats) to cultivate internal Chi and master one's own body language in order to master others via charm, confidence, and intimidation. In addition to meditation, it also involves a lot of applied psychology and rigorous study of ethical and legalist classic texts.<br /> <br /> <i>Wu Chuan</i> (&quot;Warring Fist&quot;) is used by the Vajrapani (warriors and preservers of martial cultures) as a means of combat and of self-discipline. It is the most brutal and direct style, incorporating hand to hand combat and weapons techniques in addition to the study of strategy and tactics, martial philosophy, and understanding and exploiting the mental and physical weaknesses of opponents. <br /> <br /> <i>Shen Chuan</i> (&quot;Spirit Fist&quot;) is used by the Jnani (shaman and mystic hermits) to teach students that their own body is a microcosm of the universe and Reality itself, and that as goes one, so goes the other. It involves a great deal of metaphysical and medicinal study, as well as yielding to and redirecting incoming aggressive energies. <br /> <br /> <i>Yung Chuan</i> (&quot;Ordinary Fist&quot;) is used by the Li-Hai (modernists and individualists) to adapt itself to the student's individual needs and detach them from preconceptions, with a focus on curbing violence rather than mastering it. It is a very fluid style that can incorporate nearly anything, including parkour, skateboarding, ballroom dance, gardening, fashion design, poetry, cooking, video games, and most anything else.<br /> <br /> For the Akashic Brotherhood, the mundane martial arts practiced by normal people - the various forms of kung-fu, karate, swordsmanship, wrestling, etc. - are considered &quot;Gateway Arts&quot; that can be used to lead one in to Do. This is why the Vajrapani work so hard to preserve and teach these mundane styles, and why most Akashics who came into the Tradition through that Gateway will frequently incorporate them into their Do, especially as Dharmamukti and Shastamargat.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-18T17:31:59-08:00","id":1368826,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Grumpy RPG Reviews</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1368675-what-does-do-look-like?p=1368807#post1368807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It looks like this!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cnaeIAEp2pU\" data-code=\"cnaeIAEp2pU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/cnaeIAEp2pU/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>What did I do to you? What have I done to deserve this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/899-the-free-man","name":"The Free Man","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar899_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}